Previously a truthy but malformed pacer reply could skip lease acquisition
while still proceeding to reserve budget, journal an attempt, and mutate.
Synthetic tests verify unknown codes, missing release methods, and malformed
lease timestamps stop before mutation or durable attempt creation.

On 2026-10-10, explicit reconciliation began requiring exact
`not-confirmed` and `confirmed` resolution envelopes. Previously the public
reconciliation entrypoint could accept a negative result with extra or
contradictory fields and clear an ambiguous attempt. Portable tests verify
that malformed resolutions preserve the pending journal and its recovery
evidence instead of making a write eligible for immediate replay.

On 2026-10-10, the final write-execution port began validating exact
confirmed receipts and known browser stops before advancing a checkpoint.
Previously a truthy acknowledgement from an unexpected adapter could be
trusted as a typed write result; forged provider fields were not checked
at this final boundary. Portable tests prove malformed results cannot
advance an unjournaled operation and leave persisted attempts ambiguous
rather than confirming them or allowing immediate replay.

On 2026-10-10, baseline capture began validating the outer runtime
response before pacing, mutation-budget reservation, or attempt journaling.
Previously a truthy status or a surface failure with extra fields could be
admitted as a typed baseline result, even though baseline contents were
already decoded strictly. Portable tests reject malformed first and second
captures and verify no remote write or new attempt follows.

On 2026-10-10, verifier result admission now rejects unknown outcomes,
extra response fields, invalid receipt kinds, and unrecognized browser stops
before touching an ambiguous persisted attempt. Previously an unknown
outcome could fall through to `not-confirmed`, clearing the journal and
making a duplicate retry possible. Synthetic recovery tests verify that the
attempt and checkpoint remain unchanged for malformed responses.

The same pass tightened the set-list, set-detail, and question-list verifier
read envelopes. Inner IR decoding was already strict, but truthy `ok`
lookalikes or unexpected outer fields could previously reach reconciliation.
Synthetic tests now reject these replies without creating confirmation.

On 2026-10-10, the canonical browser-write adapter began enforcing exact
runtime envelopes for Create Set and Add Question confirmations and stops.
Previously a structurally extra or malformed internal surface result could be
accepted based on a truthy `ok` property, or propagated into durable attempt
state with unknown fields. Synthetic adapter regressions prove malformed
successes and invalid stop states fail closed without issuing a receipt.

On 2026-10-10, read navigation began transferring its confirmed native
target-document origin to My Sets, Set Detail, and question inspection.
Previously a replacement page at the same URL could supply independent read
authority after navigation had been confirmed. Synthetic tests show a stale
target cannot export identity or open another document's question modal.

On 2026-10-10, Add Question bound its initial Edit readiness observation
to one positive native edit-document origin before and after the page script.
A same-route reload during that observation cannot authorize opening a form
on the replacement document, even when the old script reported "edit".
Synthetic changed-epoch and missing-origin regressions prevent form opening.

On 2026-10-10, capability probe restoration also stopped treating
mere equality with the original tab URL as proof that the page is still
owned. After a modal or drawer probe, restoring a claimed route requires a
stable native document origin both when choosing restoration and immediately
before the tab update. The legacy best-effort return from an unclaimed,
still-loading My Sets page is kept separate; it does not claim a successful
capability snapshot. Synthetic cleanup and round-trip tests remain green.

On 2026-10-10, explicit browser activation began rechecking the tab
identity, exact URL, and window ownership after Chrome acknowledges its
foreground request and before the worker focuses a window. Previously a
user-initiated redirect after the initial provider-origin check could cause
an unrelated window to be focused under a Blooket human-stop receipt.
Synthetic worker tests switch to a foreign route after activation and verify
that no window focus or successful activation receipt follows.

On 2026-10-10, capability inspection also began admitting a fresh
returned edit document when the probed set shares the original teacher tab
URL. The original epoch cannot authorize the new editor after an explicit
My Sets-to-Edit navigation, even though both URLs are identical. The host
rebinds the edit route only to a positive, changed native origin and keeps
that origin through its modal probes. Synthetic regressions verify both
normal same-URL round trips and rejection of a stale target document.

On 2026-10-10, read-only capability inspection also began pinning a
positive native source-document origin before and after permission to leave,
then rechecking it at the exact tab navigation call. This prevents a same-URL
reload from turning approval to leave one teacher editor into authority over
another document. The My Sets-to-Edit probe carries the same owned My Sets
origin into its navigation guard. Synthetic tests verify that a source reload
or unavailable document identity cannot initiate the probe.

No account capability status is inferred.

On 2026-10-10, the extension worker's read navigation also began
binding the safe-to-leave approval to one native source-document origin.
Previously the worker could validate that leaving a teacher editor was safe,
then inspect a different document reloaded at the same URL and navigate away
from it. The shared read-navigation guard now requires the caller's
preapproved native origin, rather than treating a later snapshot as fresh
approval. Synthetic worker and helper tests confirm that no tab update or
reload follows the substituted source document.

On 2026-10-10, Create Set now requires a fresh native target document
origin after its tab update and rechecks that origin across session-page
observation and the form ownership Claim. Add Question similarly refuses a
target edit page retaining the source document epoch after navigation.
Synthetic tests prove that a stale completed tab, a same-route reload during
page inspection, and a replacement during Claim cannot authorize a new
form mutation. The check does not prove ownership of live account data.

On 2026-10-10, Create Set and Add Question navigation began preserving
one positive native dashboard document origin across the permission-to-leave
check and the actual tab update. Previously a same-URL reload between the
safe-to-leave inspection and the navigation could replace the source document
with a teacher-owned editor without changing the observed URL. Portable
synthetic tests replace the native origin or withhold it; both hosts refuse to
navigate or open forms. Post-submit evidence and authenticated Blooket
acceptance remain separate, unverified gates.

On 2026-10-10, the read navigation guard added a second native document
origin check immediately before issuing its one allowed reload or navigation.
Previously the teacher could replace the current document at the same URL
after the first check, and the worker would still navigate away from the
new editor. Synthetic tests confirm that no navigation or reload follows a
changed native origin, including on an already loaded target route.

On 2026-10-10, explicit login hosting began pinning one positive native
identity-page document origin before forwarding credential input, during
prepared-state polling, and immediately before its only possible submit.
Previously a reload to the exact same `/login` URL could reuse stale proof
from another document and send credentials to a replacement form.

Synthetic tests replace the document during preparation and polling, and
reject missing, nonnumeric, and nonpositive origins before any credentials
are supplied. No login success or challenge bypass is inferred.

On 2026-10-10, the read-only capability probe began tracking a positive
native document origin separately on My Sets and on the probed edit route.
The previous URL/status checks could mix separate documents, dismiss an
unrelated Audio/question panel after a same-URL reload, or overwrite an
independently changed page during route restoration.

Synthetic tests replace the document during initial listing, modal opening,
Audio evidence, and Cancel. These cases refuse the capability snapshot and
skip foreign-document cleanup and restoration. A never-hydrated My Sets route
can still be restored before the probe has claimed any document or editor.
No actual account capability acceptance is inferred.

On 2026-10-10, session inspection began binding both stable navigation
observations to one native document origin, including the exact
`https://id.blooket.com/login` identity page. A separate read-only helper
admits only the dashboard and Blooket identity origins; the saved-data origin
helper remains dashboard-only.

A synthetic same-route reload now invalidates two otherwise identical session
replies. The emitted extension helper is also serialized and evaluated without
its module lexical scope. Neither establishes an authenticated product session.

On 2026-10-10, the extension worker's saved Set Detail path began pinning
one positive native `performance.timeOrigin` across its sidebar baseline,
metadata editor open, panel reads, and Cancel confirmation. Checking only the
edit URL previously allowed a replacement document to provide inconsistent
metadata or receive an unrelated Cancel click.

Synthetic worker regressions replace the same-route document during the
sidebar, after the editor opener, and during metadata inspection. All are
refused, and the replacement document never receives the cleanup click.
A verified live set detail read remains pending.

On 2026-10-10, the existing-question read host began preserving one native
`performance.timeOrigin` across number enumeration, modal inspection, saved
image probing, cancellation, and closure verification. Previously a reload of
an unchanged edit URL could splice two question collections together and a
best-effort Cancel could target another document.

Synthetic tests replace the document at each stage and verify that the host
refuses the scan and never cancels or probes a foreign document. Invalid or
unavailable native origins refuse even the initial enumeration. The deadline
remains authoritative; this is not evidence of live Blooket completeness.

On 2026-10-10, Add Question browser mutations began binding the exact edit
page to one positive native `performance.timeOrigin`. The former host checked
only tab URL and readiness between opener, prepared-state, submission, and
saved-question reads. A reload of the same edit route could therefore mix
separate documents, including after an uncertain Save Question click.

The host now rechecks that origin before and after every owned injected
operation. Synthetic replacements during preparation, after one submit, and
between read-back observations all fail without a second submission. Missing
native origin evidence stops before opening a mutation form. Loaded-extension
and authenticated acceptance remain pending.

On 2026-10-10, the Create Set extension host began binding the edit redirect,
its second confirmation read, and each saved-sidebar observation to one
positive native `performance.timeOrigin` on the exact edit route. A document
replacement at the same `/edit?id=...` URL previously could mix two valid
observations and fabricate a stable saved-set receipt.

Synthetic tests replace the origin before the second receipt and between
sidebar samples; both stop without retrying the mutation. Missing native
origin evidence also fails closed. No authenticated Create Set acceptance is
inferred.

On 2026-10-10, the shared publication file-lock reader switched from a
pathname `lstat`/`readFile` pair to a bounded `O_NOFOLLOW` descriptor read.
The prior sequence could follow a substituted symlink between inspection
and reading the recovery owner. Exact 4,096-byte and oversized owner tests,
as well as dangling-link refusal, preserve fail-closed lock acquisition.
The [official Node 24 filesystem API][node-nofollow], checked on 2026-10-10,
documents this flag; tests are portable, not a live account mutation.

On 2026-10-10, checkpoint persistence reads began opening the checkpoint
with `O_NOFOLLOW` and inspecting and reading the same descriptor. This removes
the pathname-based `lstat`/`readFile` substitution window and bounds checkpoint
reads to 65,536 bytes, including unexpected file growth. Synthetic filesystem
regressions cover the exact byte boundary and dangling symlink refusal. This
protects local recovery evidence without claiming live provider acceptance.

The attempt journal now follows the same bounded descriptor rule on
2026-10-10: it is read through one `O_NOFOLLOW` descriptor, not a mutable
pathname, and is limited to 65,536 bytes including later file growth.
Synthetic tests cover successful preserved attempts, oversized data, and a
dangling symbolic link. Existing journals are never silently recreated.

On 2026-10-10, hexadecimal Flight record IDs and decimal chunk IDs began
requiring canonical digit spellings. A padded `00` row previously coexisted
with row `0`, while a prefixed `01958` chunk could coexist with the pinned
`1958` chunk without duplicate detection. Both ambiguous encodings now fail
closed; synthetic fixtures retain ordinary canonical IDs.

On 2026-10-10, a synthetic Flight normalization regression exposed a direct
object/array cycle bypass: the memoized output was returned while its source
object was still resolving. The normalizer now checks active recursion before
the completed-object memo. Shared acyclic objects remain reusable. This guards
programmatic row inputs; ordinary decoded JSON cannot itself encode cycles.

An additional Flight regression on 2026-10-10 proved that ordinary JSON with
`kind: "error"` and `digest` could masquerade as an `E`-framed error row.
Only the actual validated `E` decoder now attaches an internal nonenumerable
marker. JSON lookalikes stay ordinary models; legitimate error digests retain
their exact public shape without exposing extra provider fields.

A related 2026-10-10 synthetic payload used `__proto__` as a JSON property.
The old Flight object copier invoked the inherited setter, hiding that key
from exact action-state field checks. Normalization now defines own enumerable
data properties without invoking inherited setters. Regression tests confirm
that malformed action payloads stay invalid and ordinary object prototypes are
unchanged; this does not establish a real provider response format.

The same Flight action decoder also assigned field-error entries to `{}`.
A fictional `__proto__` field name could replace the result prototype rather
than remain an enumerable own field. A 2026-10-10 regression now preserves the
original key and message as ordinary data without altering the prototype.

On 2026-10-10, a private extension-local candidate decoder was added for
My Sets server-rendered Flight data. It is gated on the specific observed
`349b82c80fc4a5a2001e609136cfed32747ba3f4` build, the client module
`52644` with chunk `1958-3beff819112074ac.js`, and exact unfiltered
`LIBRARY` props. Only normalized set IDs and titles are compared with the
visible cards; the Flight text and unrelated model props remain local to the
extension and are never returned over the browser bridge. Wrong builds retain
unknown completeness; malformed matching-build models fail closed.

A 2026-10-10 distribution regression now compiles the actual browser extension,
serializes the emitted My Sets model reader and fresh-document identity helper,
and evaluates them against a fictional DOM without their module lexical scope.
This checks shipped script closure rather than only TypeScript imports. It does
not demonstrate installation in the authenticated Chrome session.

A synthetic module-index regression on 2026-10-10 rejected repeated chunk IDs
inside one `I` record. Earlier, a correctly pinned chunk followed by a
conflicting duplicate `1958` entry could still satisfy the module identity
check. Both matching and contradictory duplicate mappings now fail closed;
provider builds and account completeness remain separately gated.

The Flight page reader accepts ID-less `:HL` resource hints without enabling
them in action-response parsing. This framing distinction was checked on
2026-10-10 against the [React Server Components documentation][react-rsc]
and [Next.js maintainer explanation][next-flight].

Tests remain synthetic. The client field named `allSets` is not an
authoritative collection-total contract. Matched nonempty collections
still report `unknown` and cannot unlock create/reconciliation baselines.
Live loaded-extension verification, a separately justified complete
nonempty collection, and Create/Add acceptance remain pending.

A second 2026-10-10 extension-worker regression checks the same native
`performance.timeOrigin` after paired DOM and model scripts. A background
replacement of the same My Sets URL now invalidates the response rather than
mixing documents; the actual assembled worker also rejects a synthetic
model/visible-card mismatch without returning raw model data. These are
portable tests, not a loaded Chrome extension observation.

The `numSets` and `numQuestions` component props are counters rather than
hardcoded zeros. A nonempty set collection can have positive counters; the
recovered My Sets client (build `86784c3d4c38fcd559c92f38fbadd7a160947de2`,
module `52644`) also uses zero defaults for nullish values in its product
limit check. On 2026-10-10, the candidate model decoder admitted null and the
exact Flight undefined marker as count fallbacks, while rejecting invalid
counter types, fractions, and overflows. This older client evidence is not
live proof of the current build, and no total establishes completeness.

On 2026-10-10, saved browser reads began requiring a new native document before
inspecting the requested route. An already loaded route now uses a
cache-bypassed reload, with the same unsaved-form guard as a route change.
A delayed complete tab reply cannot prove freshness unless its document time
origin changes.

Synthetic regressions cover stale complete replies, missing identity, expired
deadlines, manual navigation, and refusing a same-route reload while an editor
is active. These portable checks do not establish loaded-extension or real
publication acceptance; collection completeness remains a separate requirement.

On 2026-10-10, the authenticated canonical My Sets read returned five
development sets with `completeness: unknown`. The current DOM reader and
browser adapter admit complete collection evidence only for the exact empty
account banner. Thus a new Create Set on a nonempty account could be sent
without a usable differential recovery baseline; a lost reply left a visible
empty set and an attempt that ordinary reconciliation could not confirm.

Canonical publication now refuses missing baseline evidence before a new
mutation, budget reservation, or attempt journal. A second baseline disappearing
after pacing also releases the lease without writing. Tests retain legacy
baseline-less journals unchanged and never replay them.

Completion still needs an authenticated, independently justified complete
collection read, plus the normal create/add/edit acceptance. Returning
`complete` merely because cards are visible is not sufficient evidence.

On 2026-10-09, canonical text publication prepared the real Create Set form
but preserved a reconciliation-required attempt instead of asserting success.
The owned extension context confirmed a connected form, dirty watch, and
`submitted: false`; the visible form remained on Create with fixture fields.
No submission was replayed and its journal is retained.

The privacy click activates native checkbox input/change events even when the
click itself is scripted. The watch now admits only the synchronous input/change
events on the exact owned privacy target during that click, clearing the target
in `finally`. Later trusted changes, other controls, pointer and keyboard input
still relinquish ownership; regressions cover preparation and later edits.

Create ownership and Add Question opening also check computed visibility for
retained consent dialogs. Visible dialogs still stop writes. These corrections
require a freshly loaded worker and live publication verification.

The activation contract was checked on 2026-10-09 against
[WHATWG input activation][input-activation] and
[MDN Event.isTrusted][trusted-events]. Provider success is separate evidence.


On 2026-10-08, an authorized website check selected Upload by URL in the
question-image drawer and submitted a public repository PNG. No native file
picker opened. Save Question returned a third card, and a fresh Edit-page
reload retained three questions and both question images, including the
owner-selected photo in the second question.

Reopening the third card confirmed the fictional typing prompt, its accepted
answer, Is Exactly mode, 20-second limit, and rendered repository icon.
Cancel closed the unchanged editor. This establishes the website path and
persisted selector evidence only; the canonical media bridge and publication
entrypoints remain unimplemented.

On 2026-10-08, authorized Chrome creation of a private fictional development
set confirmed two visible Add Question buttons on both empty and nonempty
Edit pages. The lower button shares its immediate parent with the unique
Show all answers control; the header button does not. The previous global
uniqueness assumption prevented both question writes and capability probing.

The page runners now resolve only that observed list toolbar when exactly two
Add Question buttons exist. Unknown duplicate layouts still fail rather than
choosing the first button. Synthetic regressions reproduce the old refusal,
confirm one toolbar click, and reject duplicate Add Question controls within
the toolbar. The browser-created fictional multiple-choice question retained
its text, answers, and 15-second limit after reload; this is UI observation,
not canonical product CLI/MCP publication acceptance.

On 2026-10-08, the Create Set browser host added a post-submit route
check to reject acknowledgments from an unrelated tab destination. It still
permits a legitimate in-progress Edit redirect, but requires separate stable
read-back before reporting the new set. Synthetic tests cover both outcomes.

On 2026-10-08, Create Set preparation and submission began requiring one
unique form and one copy of each scoped title, description, and privacy
control. Duplicate forms and values outside the intended form now fail
before editing controls or clicking the submit button. The synthetic DOM
fixture covers ambiguous forms and unscoped controls, not live creation.

On 2026-10-08, Create Set redirect confirmation began requiring two
identical decoded receipts from the same owned Edit URL, separated by a
browser event-loop yield. Synthetic tests reject a missing second receipt,
a changed payload, and a route change during confirmation without converting
a one-off browser response into a publication receipt.

On 2026-10-08, the local browser broker began reserving a write-dispatch
margin for both Create Set and Add Question, matching the existing capability
cleanup budget. Queued writes with less than 9.5 seconds left on the default
ten-second deadline are settled without dispatch, rather than beginning a
form action whose caller may already have timed out. Injected-clock tests
cover refusal and a fresh singly dispatched write.

On 2026-10-08, Create Set redirect confirmation began requiring two matching,
strictly decoded Edit-page results separated by an event-loop yield. A route
change, malformed second reply, or contradictory remote ID now refuses success
rather than treating the first browser observation as a durable receipt. These
are synthetic browser-host checks, not live evidence of remote persistence.

On 2026-10-08, the extension worker started passing a generation-scoped
browser port to each write host. A synthetic reconnect during an Add Question
modal opener now prevents all later form scripts, including submission, and
blocks an obsolete response from reaching the new workspace. Browser API
calls already in flight cannot be undone, so this is a continuation safeguard
rather than atomic ownership or live acceptance.

On 2026-10-08, the Create Set Chrome host began requiring its observed
response ID to match the unique decoded `id` query field on the confirmed
Edit-page redirect. Extra reply fields, non-string IDs, ambiguous duplicate
queries, and a response claiming another set now fail closed, while opaque
identifiers with encoded spaces and slashes remain admitted. This hardens
synthetic read-back evidence but does not verify remote persistence.

On 2026-10-08, the Add Question read-back host began decoding complete
question candidates through the versioned IR contract and requiring two
matching observed values before confirming a submitted question. Malformed
number lists and unexpected reply fields cannot authorize a read-back modal;
uncertain or mismatched content triggers best-effort modal cancellation with
no mutation replay.

Synthetic tests confirm changing question contents, malformed envelopes, and
cleanup after a mismatched read fail closed. These checks do not establish a
live Blooket action response, authenticated persistence, or a remote receipt.

On 2026-10-08, the Add Question Chrome host added exact edit-route ownership
checks around its opener, readiness, preparation, one submit, and each later
read-back script. A navigation between the initial tab check and requested
edit page no longer overwrites a user-selected route. Synthetic regressions
cover stale navigation, modal opening, preparation, and read-back without a
live mutation.

On 2026-10-08, the synthetic Create Set Chrome host added a fresh tab-route
check before navigation so an independently selected edit route is not
overwritten. Exact Create-page checks now guard form preparation and submission;
late preparation replies from another route fail rather than enabling a write.

A claimed Edit-page redirect is also checked after its browser script completes
before reporting its observed set ID. Three portable regressions reproduced the
previous stale navigation, preparation, and redirect success paths. None of
these tests performs a live Blooket mutation or verifies an HTTP action body.

The current observed Blooket build is
`4e10e84779aaa361fd4310c02366e37ebee7b60d`. Keep this evidence distinct from
older captures in `reference/curated/`. Use the recovered references according
to `AGENTS.md`; never import their code into production.

Observed actions include create set, add question, and update question. Create
posts multipart fields to `/create`; add/update post to `/edit` with the set
context. The initial Flight form state is `UNSET` with message and field errors.
The private creation checkbox is omitted for a private quiz; its UI label is
inverted relative to the field name, so do not infer visibility from the name.

Sanitized synthetic payload fixtures now pin title/description, set ID, question
JSON, answer order/correctness, `mc`/`typing`, match modes, seconds, and the
inverted public/private checkbox semantics. Media-backed set/question candidates
remain unsupported before any HTTP mutation.

The real action response body was not recovered: attempts to retrieve it through
the browser tool failed. HTTP 200 and UI read-back are not a decoded response
contract. Capture and validate actual response shapes before activating HTTP
writes, and never treat `SUCCESS` inferred from client code as observed
evidence.

Bounded Flight candidate decoding was hardened on 2026-10-06 against the
recovered production runtime. IDs, JSON rows, UTF-8 byte-length `T` rows,
duplicate IDs, row/graph limits, direct `$<hex>` references, shared references,
cycles, and unsupported reference/tag kinds are now explicit and fail closed.

Action-state status is type-checked without coercion, field errors are bounded,
and malformed JSON is rejected rather than replaced with null. `E` records
expose only their bounded digest. The same-build Next action reducer establishes
that redirects arrive through `x-action-redirect`, so redirect metadata is
decoded separately and restricted to same-origin HTTPS instead of being invented
as a Flight row. Synthetic tests cover all of these cases.

Same-build action response metadata now has an explicit candidate decoder:
`text/x-component` must match exactly before Flight parsing, redirects remain
same-origin HTTPS, and revalidation flags preserve their wire-level zero/one
values. HTTP status, `SUCCESS`, redirects, revalidation, and error digests are
returned only as response evidence; the candidate shape has no confirmation or
receipt field.

Pre-mutation transport selection is also explicit. While the HTTP response
contract remains unverified, even lowerable HTTP payloads select the browser
before mutation. Unsupported HTTP operations also select browser before
mutation, while any already-ambiguous primary mutation requires reconciliation
and cannot fall back or retry.

The browser fallback now has a concrete text-only Create Set path. Its page
helper recognizes only the observed create form, uses native control setters,
revalidates the exact values immediately before one submit click, and accepts
only the observed same-origin /edit?id=... success navigation.

The Chrome host preserves session/challenge states and bounds navigation
polling. Its internal sets.create bridge command accepts only bounded
title/description/privacy fields; media, arbitrary fields, malformed surface
results, and Add Question remain fail-closed.

The local browser-service composition routes this bridge surface through the
canonical browser write execution adapter, so an observed remote set ID is
decoded again before it can become a durable receipt. External text-only
publication commands now compose through persisted execution and MCP/CLI;
no live application-driven Create Set acceptance is claimed from their
synthetic transport tests.

A private one-question quiz was created through the authorized Blooket UI and
changed from multiple choice to typing with a 15-second limit. Its final type
and time persisted after reload. Request fields were observed; the action
response body and application-driven publication were not verified.

The private test quiz remains in the account; do not delete it automatically or
commit its ID/account content. Temporary captures and logs may be cleaned
between sessions. The durable conclusions are recorded here; do not assume an
ignored fixture still exists. The configuration regression log is currently
under `.temp/readme-ui-check/full-tests.log`.

## Blockers

Acceptance remains open until the checks above run through the actual owning
product surfaces; portable foundations alone do not complete this record.

